  {
    "episode_id": 86581,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86581.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "It's landmark legislation when it comes to specifically online sex trafficking. The name of the bill is Foster, which is the Fight Online Sex Trafficking Act of 2017. I've been at this for two years. I've been fighting the good fight on human trafficking and sex trafficking since I've been in Congress now. on year six and this has been a labor of love but a pretty heavy lift because this is a mammoth piece of legislation that really goes to the heart of taking on these predatory websites like Backpackpage.com and others that are selling our women, our children, our young boys on the internet with impunity. So FOSTA has a large criminal component, which is what I have worked very hard on, working with our prosecutors, our district attorneys, our 50 state attorneys general who have gotten behind this legislation. We give prosecutors every tool in the toolbox, both by using the sex trafficking statutes and the prostitution statutes in order to go after these websites, get prosecutions, convictions, put these predatory websites, the predators behind bars or bring the websites down. And I hope, Joe, serve as a deterrent for others. So the Manhattan DA, who I worked with at length, said that this was transformational. So we've got the criminal component, and then we have the piece that we amended on, which was in my original bill two years ago, which provides civil remedies for our victims and our survivors. So this is really going to give these survivors some justice and their day in court. And then the big thing is we did what the courts have been asking for years that Congress does, which is to clarify congressional intent when it comes to the Communications Decency Act. This was a piece of legislation passed in 1996 and has been watered down and misused by Backpage.com and other, there are hundreds of these websites that are out there that are just nefarious and, as I said, exploiting the most vulnerable in our society. They've asked for us to clarify it. They've been hiding behind the immunity causes of the CDA now for years. And so we have. And we made it very clear that it is not Congress's intent for the Internet to be a red light district. And if you're committing a crime offline, you can't commit it online. If it's a crime offline, it's a crime online, too. As frankly, as sex trafficking has evolved over the last decade or more, we've seen it move from the streets to the internet. Nick Mick, which is the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children, has said there's been an 846% increase in child exploitation of sex trafficking online. So this is important legislation that we've worked on in a bipartisan way. It's had some headwinds though, certainly by big tech and others that are fighting it because there's big money behind this show. There are people making hundreds of thousands of millions of dollars on the backs of our women and children and selling them online. So it has a big criminal component, it has the civil remedies in it, and then we have the clarification in the Communications Decency Act that ought to be able to be put to good use.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
