  {
    "episode_id": 86581,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86581.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "It's been very positive from the very beginning. In fact, President Trump probably just two weeks after he took office and inauguration last year. He brought me into the Oval Office and signed an executive order on a signature piece of financial services legislation that I cared deeply about and that was really about helping low-income investors save money. We have a savings crisis in our country, and this was a major piece of legislation that I had been championing for some period of time. I've been in the White House many times talking about different policy issues. We've got a good cordial working relationship. I support the administration. I think we've been one unified Republican government moving forward and have certainly been able to get a lot accomplished whether it is in the regulatory realm, whether it's in tax reform, putting an end to the individual mandate when it comes to healthcare, which I think is something that allows more freedom and choice by individuals and doesn't tell the government telling you you have to purchase healthcare, you're going to be fined and penalized. I've had a lot of discussions. She's been to Missouri a couple of times. I've flown on Air Force One. I'm my own person, Joe, and I will tell you that I take a principled stand on issues if I disagree with the president or anyone else. I certainly will voice that. But I look for ways that we can work together, especially when it comes to these important policy issues. And I'm thrilled that today in the White House briefing room, Sarah Sanders talked about H.R. 1865 and endorsed this legislation being signed by the President. So I cannot wait to visit with him. And Ivanka Trump, this has been also a big issue for her. She tweeted out the day we were passing the legislation. And so we're all working well together, I think.",
